  "text": "2005B07 Briefly\noutline the pharmacology of droperidol, emphasising its\nmechanism of action, peri-operative use and side effects.\nList:\n·\nPC\n·\nPK\n·\nPD\nPhysicochemical:\nClass\n·\nButyrophenone\nPresentation\n·\nAqueous solution\n·\nLight-protected ampoule\nPharmacokinetic:\nAdministration\n·\nIV, IM, PO\nTime course\n·\nOnset 5-15 mins\n·\nDuration: up to 12 hours for sedative effect\nAbsorption\n·\nOral bioav 75%\nDistribution\n·\nPlasma protein binding 85-90%\n·\nV\nD\n1.5-2.5L/kg\nMetabolism\n·\nHepatic CYP450\n·\nClearance 8mL/kg/min (slow)\n·\nt\n1/2\nβ\n2 hours\nExcretion\n·\n1% unchanged in urine > faeces\n·\nMetabolites > urine\nPharmacodynamic:\nMechanisms\n·\nCentral DA\n2\nantagonist (Binds G\ni\nGPCR ->\n  ↓cAMP)\n·\n↓Afferents from chemoreceptor trigger zone to vomiting centre\n·\nα-\nadrenoceptor antagonist\nUse (dose)\n·\nPONV prevention or rescue (0.5-0.625mg in adults)\n·\nAnti-psychosis, chemical restraint )1.25-2.5mg slowly IV, q10\n  mins; max 10mg)\n·\nNeuroleptanaesthesia\nSide effects\n·\nSedation\n·\nMild hypotension\n·\nEPSE:\no\nParkinsonism\n  (tremor)\no\nAkathisia\n  (restlessness)\no\nTardive\n  dyskinesia (rhythmic voluntary movements of the tongue, face and jaw)\no\nAcute\n  dystonia (e.g. oculogyric crisis)\n·\nNeuroleptic malignant syndrome\n·\n↑QT\nC\n, ↑risk torsades de pointes\nFeedback welcome at\nketaminenightmares@gmail.com",
